      "latin": "Nē plōrā!",
      "status": "recommended",
      "period": "classical",
      "gloss": "don’t cry",
      "grammar": "nē with the present imperative; to several, Nē plōrāte!",
      "evidence": [
        "Plaut. Merc. 501: <span lang=\"la\">nē plōrā: nimis stultē facis</span>",
        "Quint. 6.1.46: <span lang=\"la\">date puerō pānem, nē plōret</span>"
      ],
      "comment": "<span lang=\"la\">Plōrāre</span> is to cry aloud, as a small child does; Quintilian’s witty advocate uses it of a child in court. <span lang=\"la\">Flēre</span> and <span lang=\"la\">lacrimāre</span> are to weep, shed tears.",
      "register": "vōx sermōnis cotīdiānī: Plaut."
    },
